.hermes/

# The dispatch loop's own working papers: run briefs, handovers, the product-review inbox,
# ledger and plan. They describe what was found about the live deployment, and this repo is
# public — one `git add -A` must not be able to publish them.
.dispatch/

# Module 3.2 — MCP runtime state. The curated corpus under .memory/reference/ IS committed; the
# storage database and its audit log are run output and are never committed.
.memory/storage.db
.memory/storage.db-wal
.memory/storage.db-shm
.memory/storage-audit.log

# The beta-tester's own run output: the screenshots the browser server captures are evidence a
# report cites, not source. The browser journal beside them IS committed, as the gate journal is:
# it is the record of what the server was asked to do and what it refused.
.memory/beta-evidence/

# Build and test caches — local run output, never committed.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Claude Code's per-machine settings file; the shared .claude/settings.json is committed.
.claude/settings.local.json

# The red team's run output and its working probes. This repository is public: the prompt set
# (eval/red-team-prompts.md) is the threat model and stays committed, but the raw evidence — exact
# commands, observed outputs, the grant-widening and journal-tamper scripts — is run output, and one
# `git add -A` must not be able to publish a working exploit against the harness. The scheduled pass
# that produces this evidence lives outside the repo (~/.hermes/profiles/dev/redteam/).
eval/red-team-results.md
eval/red-team/rt_grant_widen.py
eval/red-team/rt_grant_widen_files.py
eval/red-team/rt_journal_tamper.py
